2. Linux administration and troubleshooting
Question 8. A production Linux server is slow. Walk me through your first 10 minutes.
INTERVIEW-READY ANSWER
I establish scope and start time, then inspect load and processes with `uptime`, `top` and `ps`; memory and swap with
`free -h` and `vmstat`; disk and inode usage with `df -h` and `df -i`; disk I/O with `iostat -xz 1` if available; and sockets
with `ss -s`. I inspect application and system logs using `journalctl`. I compare with normal baselines, identify the
constrained resource or failing dependency, make a controlled change and verify recovery rather than killing processes
blindly.
uptime
top
free -h
df -h
df -i
ps aux --sort=-%cpu | head -n 6
ps aux --sort=-%mem | head -n 6
ss -s
journalctl -p err -n 50
LIKELY FOLLOW-UP
Which commands distinguish CPU, memory, disk capacity, inode and disk I/O problems?
Question 9. The application cannot write logs, but `df -h` shows free space. What else do
you check?
INTERVIEW-READY ANSWER
I check inode exhaustion with `df -i`, directory permissions with `ls -ld` and `namei -l`, ownership with `stat`, mount
options with `findmnt`, whether the filesystem is read-only, quotas and the service's effective user. A filesystem can have
free bytes but no free inodes, or the application may lack write permission.
Question 10. A service fails after a reboot. How do you investigate?
INTERVIEW-READY ANSWER
I check `systemctl status <service>` and `journalctl -u <service> -b`, inspect the unit using `systemctl cat`, and verify
dependencies, configuration syntax, environment files, permissions, mounts and ports. I confirm whether it should start
automatically before enabling it. After fixing the cause I restart and verify the process and application endpoint.
Question 11. A disk is full. How do you recover safely?
INTERVIEW-READY ANSWER
I identify the mount with `df -hT`, check inodes with `df -i`, locate large directories with `du -xhd1 /path | sort -h`, and
inspect logs, caches, container layers and deleted-but-open files using `lsof +L1`. I confirm retention policy before
cleanup. I use log rotation or the service's safe truncation procedure rather than deleting an active log blindly, then verify
space and fix the cause.
Question 12. SSH to 20 servers suddenly fails. How do you isolate the issue?
INTERVIEW-READY ANSWER
I test a known-good and affected host, then check DNS, route, TCP 22 connectivity, SSH daemon status,
firewall/security-group rules, authorized keys, `.ssh` permissions, disk space and authentication logs. I use console or
approved out-of-band access if necessary. I avoid weakening SSH security across the fleet before identifying the cause.
Question 13. Find the process listening on port 8080.
INTERVIEW-READY ANSWER
I use `sudo ss -lntp | grep ':8080'` to identify the listening socket and process. I can also use `sudo lsof -i :8080`. Then I
inspect the PID and service before making any change.
sudo ss -lntp | grep ':8080'
sudo lsof -i :8080
ps -fp <PID>
Question 14. A script works manually but fails in cron. Why?
INTERVIEW-READY ANSWER
Cron has a limited environment and may use a different PATH, shell, working directory and permissions. I use absolute
paths, set required variables, log stdout and stderr, verify the cron user's permissions and test with the same user and
environment. I then inspect cron logs or the system journal.
