Question 46. Deployment succeeds but users see the old version. How do you
troubleshoot?
INTERVIEW-READY ANSWER
I check Deployment image, ReplicaSets and Pods, confirm the running digest, verify Service selectors, EndpointSlices,
Ingress or load balancer routing and DNS. I check whether a mutable tag was reused and whether traffic reaches another
cluster or namespace.
Question 47. Explain startup, readiness and liveness probes.
INTERVIEW-READY ANSWER
Startup probes allow slow applications time to initialize before liveness and readiness checks begin. Readiness determines
whether a Pod receives traffic; failure removes it from ready endpoints without restarting it. Liveness detects a stuck or
unhealthy container and can trigger restart. I configure probes to reflect real application health and startup time.
Question 48. A Kubernetes node becomes NotReady. What do you do?
INTERVIEW-READY ANSWER
I inspect node conditions and events, kubelet and container runtime health, CPU, memory, disk pressure, network and
control-plane connectivity. I assess affected workloads and PodDisruptionBudgets before draining or replacing the node. I
follow the cluster node-replacement procedure and verify workloads reschedule.
Question 49. Deployment vs StatefulSet vs DaemonSet—when do you use each?
INTERVIEW-READY ANSWER
Deployment is for stateless replicas such as APIs. StatefulSet is for workloads requiring stable identity and often persistent
storage. DaemonSet runs a Pod on each eligible node, commonly for logging, monitoring or networking agents.
Question 50. A Service has no endpoints. What do you check?
INTERVIEW-READY ANSWER
I compare Service selectors with Pod labels, check Pod readiness, inspect Endpoints or EndpointSlices, namespace and
port/targetPort, and readiness probes. A Service with no matching ready Pods cannot route traffic.
Question 51. How would you safely upgrade a Kubernetes cluster?
INTERVIEW-READY ANSWER
I review supported version skew, release notes, deprecated APIs, add-on/CNI compatibility, capacity, PDBs and backups. I
validate in non-production, upgrade control plane and then worker nodes in batches using cordon and drain. I monitor
system Pods, node readiness, workloads, ingress, storage and metrics, with a recovery plan.
Question 52. A Helm upgrade fails. How do you recover?
INTERVIEW-READY ANSWER
I inspect `helm status`, `helm history`, values and Kubernetes events. I render templates with `helm template` and check
immutable-field changes, missing secrets, RBAC and readiness. If the prior release is healthy, I use `helm rollback
<release> <revision>` and verify the app, then fix and test the chart.
Question 53. ConfigMap vs Secret. Is a Kubernetes Secret encrypted?
INTERVIEW-READY ANSWER
ConfigMap is for non-confidential configuration; Secret is intended for sensitive values. Base64 encoding is not encryption.
I enable encryption at rest, restrict RBAC, avoid logging secret values and prefer external secrets management where
required.
9. AWS cloud and architecture
Question 54. Design a highly available AWS web application.
INTERVIEW-READY ANSWER
I would use a VPC across at least two Availability Zones, public subnets for an internet-facing load balancer and private
subnets for application workloads. Security groups allow only required flows. Data services use appropriate managed or
replicated options. I add autoscaling, health checks, monitoring, backups, encryption, IAM roles and IaC. The exact
architecture depends on traffic, state, compliance and budget.
Question 55. EC2 is running but SSH fails. What do you check?
INTERVIEW-READY ANSWER
I verify instance state, address and route path, then security group TCP 22 from the trusted source, subnet route table,
internet gateway or VPN/bastion, NACLs, OS firewall, sshd, user/key permissions and authentication logs. For private
instances I use approved bastion, VPN or Systems Manager access.
Question 56. An EC2 instance has no internet access. How do you troubleshoot?
