INTERVIEW-READY ANSWER
I identify whether it is in a public or private subnet. A public subnet needs a route to an Internet Gateway and a public
address or valid egress path. A private subnet commonly uses a NAT Gateway in a public subnet. I check route tables, NAT
health, security groups, NACLs, DNS and OS routes.
Question 57. Security Groups vs Network ACLs?
INTERVIEW-READY ANSWER
Security Groups are stateful and attached to network interfaces/resources; return traffic is automatically allowed. NACLs
are stateless and apply at subnet boundaries, so inbound and outbound rules must both permit traffic. Security Groups
have allow rules; NACLs support allow and deny. I use Security Groups for workload-level controls.
Question 58. An S3 bucket is accidentally public. What do you do?
INTERVIEW-READY ANSWER
I restrict public access immediately through incident procedure, preserve evidence and identify data exposure scope. I
review bucket policy, ACLs, Block Public Access, access logs and CloudTrail, notify security/data owners and remediate
with least privilege and automated checks.
Question 59. How do you store application secrets in AWS?
INTERVIEW-READY ANSWER
I use Secrets Manager or Parameter Store with KMS encryption and grant access through a least-privilege IAM role. I avoid
secrets in code, AMIs, Docker images, user data or plain-text pipeline variables. I rotate credentials and audit access.
Question 60. AWS bill increases by 40%. How do you investigate?
INTERVIEW-READY ANSWER
I compare periods in Cost Explorer by service, region, account and usage type. I inspect EC2 hours and sizes, EBS,
snapshots, NAT data processing, data transfer, load balancers, logs and S3. I distinguish expected growth from waste,
apply rightsizing/schedules/lifecycle policies and set budgets and alerts.
Question 61. How do you design backup and disaster recovery?
INTERVIEW-READY ANSWER
I agree RPO and RTO with the business, identify data and dependencies, define backup frequency, retention, encryption
and cross-account/region copies, and test restores. I use IaC to recreate infrastructure and choose pilot-light,
warm-standby or multi-site based on criticality and cost. A backup is not proven until restored.
10. Terraform and Infrastructure as Code
Question 62. Terraform plan wants to destroy and recreate an existing EC2 instance. What
do you do?
INTERVIEW-READY ANSWER
I do not apply immediately. I inspect which argument forces replacement, compare configuration with state and real
infrastructure, and check provider changes, resource addressing, moved blocks and manual drift. If unexpected, I stop and
correct the configuration or state mapping. I obtain review before destructive changes.
Question 63. Someone manually changed an AWS security group outside Terraform. What
happens?
INTERVIEW-READY ANSWER
That is configuration drift. Terraform refreshes remote information during planning and may propose changes to restore
the configuration declared in code. I inspect the plan and decide whether the manual change was intentional. If required, I
update Terraform; otherwise I apply the reviewed plan to restore the desired state.
Question 64. What is Terraform state and why protect it?
INTERVIEW-READY ANSWER
State maps Terraform resource addresses to real infrastructure objects and stores attributes needed for planning. It can
contain sensitive data, so I use encryption, strict access control, versioning and secure remote storage. Team workflows
use supported remote backends and locking; state files are never committed to Git.
Question 65. Two engineers run Terraform apply simultaneously. How do you prevent state
corruption?
INTERVIEW-READY ANSWER
A supported remote backend can lock state during write operations. I use locking and never disable it casually. If a lock is
stale, I verify no operation is active and only force-unlock with the exact lock ID under the team's procedure.
Question 66. Plan vs apply?
