18. Final mixed production scenarios
Question 110. At 2 AM, all API instances are unhealthy. What do you do first?
INTERVIEW-READY ANSWER
I acknowledge the alert, establish whether the scope is one service, zone or shared dependency, check load balancer
health, recent deployments, application logs, resource metrics, network and database. I notify on-call, mitigate with the
safest action such as rollback or dependency recovery, verify user-facing health and then document timeline and
prevention.
Question 111. QA works but production Kubernetes deployment fails. What differences do
you compare?
INTERVIEW-READY ANSWER
I compare image digest, chart and values, namespace, ConfigMaps and Secrets, service account/RBAC, resource
requests/limits, network policies, ingress, DNS, external dependencies, storage class and cluster versions. I inspect
production events and rendered manifests rather than guessing.
Question 112. A pipeline has no rollback stage. What would you propose?
INTERVIEW-READY ANSWER
I define rollback for application and data separately. For stateless workloads it may redeploy the last known-good
immutable image or Helm revision. Database changes need backward-compatible migrations because rolling back code
may not reverse schema. I add health gates, deployment metadata, documented rollback, approval and verification, then
test it.
Question 113. A team wants to deploy directly from a developer laptop. What risks do you
explain?
INTERVIEW-READY ANSWER
It reduces traceability and repeatability and may bypass review, tests, scans, artifact versioning, approvals and audit
records. I recommend deployment through CI/CD from a reviewed commit using an immutable artifact, controlled
permissions and an auditable release record.
Question 114. A repetitive task is undocumented but you are asked to automate it. What
do you do?
INTERVIEW-READY ANSWER
I observe the current process and speak with owners to identify inputs, outputs, approvals, failure cases and rollback. I
document it, build a small safe version, test in non-production, add logging and error handling, and ask the process owner
to validate. Automating an unclear process can make mistakes happen faster.
